-\toolname rests on two ideas: that every class of heterozygous evidence a read carries belongs in one haplotype model, provided that each observation is weighted by how far it can be trusted, and that a phaser should indicate where its own output is unreliable. The first extends an observation made independently for methylation\cite{akbari2021nanomethphase,fu2024methphaser,akbari2023parentoforigin} and for SVs\cite{lin2022longphase,holt2024hiphase}, namely that SNVs alone leave haplotype blocks stranded wherever heterozygosity is low. LongHap also uses all four classes, but adds methylation only after sequence phasing, to bridge the blocks it leaves\cite{pfennig2026longhap}; in \toolname, methylation and sequence evidence are weighed together as the phase is built. The evidence classes did not contribute alike. Methylation and SVs lengthened blocks with little change in the switch error rate, and after correction SNV+5mC phasing had a switch error rate similar to that of SNV-only phasing with 8\% longer blocks at 60$\times$. Indels contributed most of the extra contiguity and most of the extra error: without correction, four-class co-phasing raised block N50 by 75\% (5.1 versus 2.9~Mb) and the SNV switch error rate from 0.034\% to 0.043\%. The SNV switch error rate of \whatshap also rose, by 17--20\%, when it phased indels, which is consistent with difficulties introduced by indel evidence, although shared limitations in how both tools represent or model indels remain possible. Correction reduced the indel-associated increase by 41--67\%, leaving four-class co-phasing with 43\% longer blocks than corrected SNV-only phasing at a switch error rate (0.027\%) below that of uncorrected SNV-only phasing, although still above that of corrected SNV-only phasing (0.023\%). The choice between the two configurations is therefore one of contiguity against accuracy, and more accurate indel calling\cite{zheng2026accelerated,kolesnikov2024local} would narrow it.
+\toolname rests on two ideas: that every class of heterozygous evidence a read carries belongs in one haplotype model, provided that each observation is weighted by how far it can be trusted, and that a phaser should withhold phase where its own output is unreliable. The first extends observations made for methylation\cite{akbari2021nanomethphase,fu2024methphaser,akbari2023parentoforigin} and for SVs\cite{lin2022longphase,holt2024hiphase} that SNVs alone leave blocks stranded where heterozygosity is low; unlike LongHap, which adds methylation after sequence phasing to bridge the blocks it leaves\cite{pfennig2026longhap}, \toolname weighs methylation and sequence evidence together as the phase is built. The evidence classes did not contribute alike. Methylation lengthened blocks at a similar switch error rate, whereas indels contributed most of the additional contiguity and most of the additional error, as they did for \whatshap; this is consistent with difficulties introduced by indel evidence, although limitations that both tools share in representing or modelling indels remain possible. Joint phasing is therefore a choice between contiguity and accuracy, which correction narrows and more accurate indel calling\cite{zheng2026accelerated,kolesnikov2024local} would narrow further.
 
-The second idea turns phase from a binary state into a graded one, but only part of that gradation reaches the output. \toolname writes the phasing entropy and the two weighted vote totals of every phased variant to the VCF, giving downstream analyses a per-variant indication of how equivocal the phasing evidence was, in the spirit of the posteriors reported by \hapcut\cite{edge2017hapcut2} and by statistical phasers\cite{hofmeister2023shapeit5}. Entropy alone is nonetheless an incomplete guide to error. Of the benchmark heterozygotes that the network unphased, 22--43\% had an entropy of exactly 0, that is, unanimous but thin support that a high-entropy threshold alone would not flag, although a threshold on support counts might. The network, which reads the votes of neighbouring variants, unphased these sites, but its phase-error score is used only to decide what to unphase and is not reported. We have not yet measured how well either quantity is calibrated against the truth set, and until that is done the entropy is best read as a flag for equivocal evidence, not as a probability that the phase is wrong.
+The second idea replaces a binary phased-or-unphased state with a graded one, but only partly. \toolname writes the phasing entropy and weighted vote totals of every phased variant, in the spirit of the posteriors of \hapcut\cite{edge2017hapcut2} and statistical phasers\cite{hofmeister2023shapeit5}, yet many removed benchmark heterozygotes had an entropy of exactly 0, unanimous but thin support that a high-entropy threshold alone would not flag. The network reads the evidence across a window rather than the likelihood of the phasing model, as \hapcut's pruning does, but whether it withholds better than simpler rules (entropy or support-count thresholds, random removal or \hapcut's confidence) at matched numbers of withheld variants has not been tested, and its phase-error score is neither calibrated nor reported. Because correction only withholds phase, part of its gain follows by construction; Margin's lower switch error rate at 10$\times$, which coincided with three-fold shorter blocks, shows why error must be read alongside contiguity. What correction withheld lay mostly outside the regions that the assembly-derived truth set can assess, where phase cannot currently be verified, and its benefit carried over to HiFi data from the same individual.
 
