-Our comparison of benchmarks also bears on how phasers are evaluated. Scored against GIAB v4.2.1\cite{wagner2022giab} and against the benchmark derived from the complete diploid T2T-HG002 assembly\cite{hansen2026q100,giab2025q100}, the same phased VCFs gave different error magnitudes and, for some tools, a different ordering: at 30--60$\times$ the ratio between the highest and lowest switch error rate widened from 1.2--1.3-fold to 4.3--4.8-fold, and \hapcut went from making fewer switch errors than \whatshap with indel co-phasing to making more. Two effects combine. \whatshap and \hapcut made more switch errors under the T2T-based benchmark at 10--50$\times$ (about 1.6-fold more at 10$\times$), which would be consistent with wrong linkages from mis-mapped reads in the repeat-rich sequence it adds, although this has not been tested by stratification. Conversely, under v4.2.1 all tools converged on a floor of 1,600--2,200 switch errors from 30$\times$ upwards, and corrected \toolname made fewer switch errors under the T2T-based benchmark at every coverage (1,518 versus 1,820 at 10$\times$; 496 versus 1,676 at 60$\times$). Similar aggregate counts do not show that the tools share the same discordances, and we have not intersected error locations or separated the regions and alleles common to both benchmarks from those specific to one. Benchmark choice therefore changed the magnitude of disagreement and the separation between methods, but the contributions of regional coverage, variant representation and phase discordance remain unresolved; the three SNV-only tools ranked identically under both. Published comparisons of phasers\cite{patterson2015whatshap,edge2017hapcut2,shafin2021margin,holt2024hiphase,fu2024methphaser,lin2022longphase} were scored against truth sets confined to sequence in which reads map reliably, where phasers differ least. Because the T2T-HG002-derived benchmark is still being refined\cite{giab2025q100}, comparisons should report both truth sets and stratify switch errors by genomic context\cite{dwarshuis2024stratifications,nurk2022t2t,liao2023pangenome}.
+Several limitations bound these conclusions. All data come from one individual, HG002; the network was trained on its nanopore data, with labels from the evaluation benchmark and windows from the same down-sampling replicates, and the genome-wide rates include the 17 chromosomes that contributed training windows (Methods). The gains therefore require confirmation on the held-out chromosomes, on an independent individual and benchmark with frozen weights and thresholds, and above 20$\times$\todo{The training-label code matches truth records by position only, without REF/ALT, accepts unphased truth genotypes and compares phase within query phase sets alone (\texttt{prepare\_gnn\_data\_10.py}:343, 440). Audit the training records for allele mismatches, unphased truth and truth-block crossings; if any are found, correct the labels and retrain}. Values at 30--60$\times$ and on HiFi come from single replicates, and the HiFi runs appear to share one call set. Benchmark choice changed the magnitude of disagreement and the separation between methods\cite{wagner2022giab,hansen2026q100}; the contributions of regional coverage, variant representation and phase discordance remain unresolved, and comparisons should report both truth sets and stratify errors by genomic context\cite{dwarshuis2024stratifications}. For indels, SVs and 5mC we report how many were phased, not whether they were phased correctly, and corrections to SV and 5mC phase are unsupervised. 5mC co-phasing was assessed only on the autosomes of a male lymphoblastoid cell line\cite{fu2024methphaser}, and the comparison with MethPhaser awaits verification. \toolname assumes a diploid genome with balanced allele fractions.
 
